Durzynski then turns immediately to dilution, because ownership in one round is only the beginning of the story. In his example, $\begin{aligned}
f_A &= \frac{5}{5+5} = 50\%, \\
f_B &= \frac{10}{15+10} = 40\%.\end{aligned}$ The second round dilutes the holders from the first round. If the first investor held $50\%$, then after the later $40\%$ round that stake becomes $\begin{aligned}
\omega_{A,\mathrm{after}\ B} &= 0.50(1-0.40)=0.30, \\
\omega_{\mathrm{VC,total}} &= 0.30 + 0.40 = 0.70.\end{aligned}$ So after the two rounds the venture investors together own about $70\%$ of the business.

