The income statement, or profit-and-loss statement, is the part everyone thinks they know: show revenue, subtract cost of goods sold, and compute what remains. In the lecture’s simplified operating language, $\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{GrossMargin}_t &= R_t - \mathrm{COGS}_t, \\
\mathrm{OperatingProfit}_t &= R_t - \mathrm{COGS}_t - \mathrm{DeptExp}_t.\end{aligned}$ Here $R_t$ is revenue, $\mathrm{COGS}_t$ is cost of goods sold, and $\mathrm{DeptExp}_t$ is the rollup of departmental expenses: sales, marketing, R&D, G&A, staffing, and the rest of the operating structure.

